I was hosting my first dinner at  Stanford already and insecure hostess  this was a pretty stressful occasion for  me I had managed spaghetti and meatballs  which will call passable and I had  purchased and obscene quantity of cheap  trader joe's wine in an attempt to hide  my poor cooking we were well into our  third bottle when one of my new friends  Jennifer turned and said can I ask you a  question sure I said I hoped I wasn't  going to be anything about the cooking  because then I'd have to admit that  pretty much everything in front of us  had come out of a box instead she said  Anna you and Jason have been married for  two years what do you think is the  secret to love my heart was racing as  the room turned and stared at me first I  definitely do not believe that there's a  secret and secondly I also did not  believe that being married for two years  qualified me as any sort of relationship  expert I can't remember what I said but  I can guarantee that at that time it was  neither true nor helpful  it was one of those moments where I  really really wished I had an answer I  spent a lot of time thinking about this  question and thanks to a lot of you who  came to me to ask for love advice you  know who you are Jackie I'm here in  front of you one and a half years later  with what I think is a true and helpful  answer love is a wave with highs and  lows some of our deepest lows are  conflict and although our relationship  might appear perfect on the outside  Jason and I are no different for most  couples one of our deepest lows happened  five years ago my mom was diagnosed with  breast cancer I was devastated I called  Jason and he came home from work  immediately he bought me a train ticket  from New York to Boston and he walked me  to Penn Station at four in the morning  the next few months were challenging to  say the least I commuted from Boston to  New York trying to care for my mother  and also trying to do a good job in my  work as an investment banking analyst  one weekend my mom seemed to be doing  well so I decided that I was going to go  home and catch up on my work in New York  Jason was on vacation across the country  and he decided to surprise me by coming  back early of course I totally agreed to  go to a short dinner somewhere in the  neighborhood 30 minutes later we found  ourselves still stuck in a cab our cab  driver had committed the eighth cardinal  sin known only to New Yorkers he had  taken us through Times Square  45 minutes later we finally arrived at a  charming cubby hole in the west village  that charmingly kept its reservations on  pen and paper and charmingly had lost  our reservation an hour and a half later  we were finally seated two hours later  we had obtained bread and water with the  promise of wine on the way two and a  half hours later was when I completely  lost it I got mad at Jason I yelled at  him and I accused him of the most  heinous of offences trying to do  something nice for me how dare he  despite my egregious behavior we were  married in 2012 this is one of our  winning photos although I remembered  talking through this issue I still  wasn't sure why Jason forgave me so a  few weeks ago I called him to ask him  about this and he said at the time you  had an aura of gloom around you  seriously an aura of gloom yes an aura  of gloom I blame the context I didn't  blame you we had had so many happy  memories and I could see a path forward  to many more happy memories to come love  is a wave with highs and lows when I was  researching love i came across the work  of chip knee a psychologist who studies  how we behave in relationships  particularly during conflict as it turns  out we all have these mental models that  deeply impact how we behave even though  we don't realize the first is this  concept of destiny the idea of meant to  be we're all familiar with this concept  the thought that there is one person out  there who we're meant to find I blame  Hollywood and Disney the second is the  idea of growth this is based on Carol  Dweck's work on mindset Carol says that  people who have a growth mindset tend to  embrace failure they view it as an  opportunity to learn people with a fixed  mindset instead view failure is it ding  against their intelligence and they stop  trying  it turns out that it's remarkably  similar in relationships people who are  high on the growth spectrum tend to view  conflict as inevitable as something that  needs to be worked through and even as  an opportunity to become closer to your  partner people who are low on the  spectrum give up very easily destiny is  much more complicated early on it can  completely kill a relationship after all  you wouldn't be having a petty squabble  with your loved one about where to go  for dinner on Friday night if you were  meant to be with them right later on  it's much more complicated on the one  hand it can make you work harder to  salvage this relationship that you're  meant to be in but it can also cause  denial and avoidance of problems what's  really powerful to me about all of this  is it is completely in our minds we can  control it if we choose to put aside  this concept of destiny and instead  embrace the idea of growth in the idea  of working through the lows we can all  have happier and healthier relationships  when I thought of couples who did this  well I immediately thought of my  husband's grandparents grandma Aiden  pops have been married for 67 years two  years ago we celebrated their 65th  anniversary it was so moving to see how  happy they were to have their kids their  grandkids and their great grandkids all  assembled in one room this is a photo of  them in 1949 and they got married and  this is another photo more recently  taken right before the party when I call  to ask them what they thought about love  and relationships I learned some  interesting things first I learned that  my husband had sneakily been talking to  pops almost since the day we got married  and getting his advice I would argue  that jason has been employing Pops's  advice to great effect in our  relationship according to pops there are  three little words that can make for a  successful marriage and they are yes my  dear  but in all seriousness grama ADA did  give me some real material to work with  she said we've shared some incredible  highs the birth of our children as one  example but we've also shared some  really deep lows in particular the  health problems they've experienced  recently as they've gotten older at any  moment she said you have the choice to  stay in a relationship or to leave it  particularly when times get tough maybe  I'm a little bit higher on destiny than  I'd like to admit but I was surprised I  thought that they were going to talk  about love and the meant to be and  instead both of them just talked about  how hard it was this wasn't at all what  I was expecting to hear love is a wave  with highs and lows my wish for all of  us sitting in this room today is that we  can put aside this idea of destiny that  we can stop thinking about the meant to  be and instead embrace what we have  today if we embrace the work that the  lows take to get through we can all have  healthier and happier friendships family  relationships and yes even romantic  relationships with our loved ones thank  you
 

